course: everyday
lesson: 6
title: Skating and sports safety
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On skates or a skateboard the most common injury is a broken wrist, because people throw out a hand when they fall; wrist guards have a hard plate that slides along the ground instead of the bones taking the blow.
explain: So wear the full set: a helmet, wrist guards, elbow pads and knee pads, and learn to fall forward onto your pads.
explain: Before sport, warm up with a few minutes of gentle running and moving: your muscles get warmer, more blood flows to them and they stretch and react better, so they are less likely to be strained.
explain: Drink water, use the right gear for each sport (a mouthguard, shin pads, goggles), and if your head gets a knock, stop and tell an adult: «when in doubt, sit it out».
explain: A girl in Calgary heading for the ice rink puts on her helmet and gloves, warms up her legs, and skates only on ice the rink staff say is open.
explain: A girl in Minnesota went roller skating without wrist guards, fell backwards onto her hands and broke both wrists.
explain: A boy in Dublin got a hard knock to the head in a rugby match and kept playing; he got a second knock and was much more badly hurt than if he had stopped after the first.
fact: Wrist injuries are among the most common injuries in skating and skateboarding.
fact: Wrist guards, elbow pads, knee pads and a helmet protect the parts that hit the ground in a fall.
fact: A warm-up makes muscles warmer and readier to work, so they are less likely to be strained.
fact: A warm-up is a few minutes of gentle movement before harder exercise.
fact: A cool-down of slow movement after exercise helps the body calm down gradually.
fact: You lose water when you sweat, so you should drink during and after sport, even before you feel thirsty.
fact: A mouthguard protects the teeth in sports like hockey, rugby and basketball.
fact: Shin pads protect the shins in football.
fact: Anyone who gets a knock to the head in sport should stop playing and tell an adult.
fact: A concussion is a hurt to the brain caused by a knock to the head; its signs include headache, dizziness, feeling sick and confusion.
fact: Pain is a signal that something may be hurt, so you should not keep playing through sharp pain.
fact: Broken wrists are the most common injury for skaters, and wrist guards help prevent them.
fact: Anyone who gets a knock to the head in sport should stop playing and be checked, even if they feel fine.
exercise: Why do skaters often break their wrists? | They put out a hand to catch themselves in a fall | Skates are too heavy for the wrists to carry all day long | Their wrists get cold on the ice | They hold the skates the wrong way
exercise: How does a wrist guard protect you? | Its hard plate slides and takes the blow | It makes your hand heavier so you fall less | It keeps your hand warm | It makes you skate faster
exercise: Which set of gear is best for inline skating? | Helmet, wrist guards, elbow and knee pads | Just a helmet, done up tightly under the chin | Just knee pads | A cap and gloves
exercise: Why should you warm up before sport? | Warm muscles work better and strain less | It tires you out so you play slower and more carefully | It makes your bones longer | It is only for grown-ups
exercise: What is a good warm-up? | A few minutes of gentle running and moving | Sitting still for ten minutes to save energy for the game | Sprinting as fast as you can | Eating a big meal
exercise: Why should you drink water during sport? | You lose water when you sweat | Water makes your muscles heavier | Water stops you sweating | It is only to cool your mouth
exercise: What does a mouthguard protect? | Your teeth | Your nose | Your ears | Your eyes and eyebrows
exercise: What do shin pads protect in football? | The fronts of your lower legs | Your knees from bending the wrong way | Your toes | Your thighs
exercise: A player bumps her head hard during a game. What should she do? | Stop playing and tell an adult | Keep playing if she can still run | Drink some water and carry on | Play on and see if it gets worse
exercise: Which of these can be a sign of concussion? | Headache, dizziness and confusion | A bruise on the knee from a fall on the grass | Feeling hungry | Sweating on a hot day
exercise: What does «when in doubt, sit it out» mean? | If you might have hurt your head, stop playing | Always sit down at half time | Sit on the bench whenever you feel a bit tired or bored | Never play sport at all
exercise: Why should you not keep playing through sharp pain? | Pain warns you something may be hurt | Pain makes you stronger | Pain goes away faster if you keep playing hard | Sharp pain is always nothing
exercise: What is a cool-down? | Slow, easy movement after exercise | A cold shower before sport | Drinking ice water fast | Stopping suddenly and lying down
exercise: When you feel yourself falling on skates, which way is usually best to go? | Forward onto your pads | Backwards onto your head | Sideways onto a stiff arm | Straight down onto your tailbone
test: A boy in Brisbane skates with a helmet but no wrist guards. Which injury is he most at risk of? | A broken wrist | A broken nose | A sprained neck | A broken toe
test: Why might a sprinter pull a muscle if she runs fast without warming up? | Her cold muscles are stiff and strain easily | Her shoes are too new | She is too rested | Cold muscles are actually stronger than warm ones, so it is not that
test: Which is true? | Drink during sport, even before you feel thirsty | Drinking during sport always gives you a stomach ache | Sweating does not lose any water | Water makes you play worse
test: After a hard tackle a rugby player feels dizzy and confused. What should happen? | He leaves the game and an adult checks him | He sits out one minute and then goes straight back on | He drinks juice and keeps playing | He walks it off and plays on
test: Why do ice hockey players wear mouthguards? | Pucks and sticks can knock out teeth | Mouthguards help them breathe | Mouthguards keep their teeth warm | They help them shout louder
test: Lily's ankle hurts sharply after she lands badly in netball. What is wisest? | Stop playing and get it checked | Keep playing so it does not stiffen | Jump on it to test it | Hide it so she is not taken off
test: What would happen to your muscles during a good warm-up? | They get warmer and more blood flows to them | They get colder and stiffer, ready to work hard | They shrink a little | Nothing at all changes
test: Why are elbow and knee pads useful for a beginner on a skateboard? | Elbows and knees often hit the ground | They make the board go straighter | They help you balance on one foot | They make you lighter
test: A skater falls forward and slides on her knee pads and wrist guards. Why does it hurt less? | The hard pads slide and take the blow | Sliding makes her fall slower in the air | Pads make the ground softer | She was lucky, pads do nothing
test: skating tmrw, what pads do i need | Helmet, wrist guards, elbow and knee pads | Just a helmet is plenty if you go slowly and carefully | Only knee pads | Gloves and a scarf
test: got hit in the head at footy, feel dizzy but wanna keep playing | Stop playing and tell an adult | Keep playing, it will pass | Drink water and carry on | Play in goal instead, so you run less
test: why do we have to warm up, so boring | Warm muscles are less likely to get hurt | It makes the game last longer | Cold muscles are stronger | It's only for adults with stiff old backs
test: playing hockey, coach says mouthguard, why | It protects your teeth | It helps you breathe faster | It keeps your mouth warm | It makes you run quicker
test: got hit in the head at football, bit dizzy but coach needs me | Stop playing and tell the coach you are dizzy | Keep playing so the team does not lose the match | Shake your head to clear it | Drink water and go back on
test: skating at the park, wrist guards are so babyish | Wear them, falls on hands often break wrists | Skip them, they only matter for babies | Wear them only on your ankles | Wear them only when it rains
test: Ollie says if u can stand up after a head knock ur fine. right? | No, a head injury can be serious even if you can stand | Yes, standing shows the brain is fine | Yes, only blood means it is serious | Yes, dizziness always goes in a minute
test: did u hear Zoe broke BOTH wrists skating and has to have her mum feed her for a month | The real lesson is to wear wrist guards when skating; the detail may be a bit blown up | The real lesson is that skating is only for boys | It proves wrist guards are useless | It means helmets alone protect every part of you
test: our coach's mate got a knock in rugby, kept playing, and they're saying he forgot his own name | Whatever the details, a knock to the head means stop playing and be checked | It is just a story, so it is fine to keep playing after a knock | It proves only rugby players get concussion | It means you only stop if you forget your name
test: Josh threw up after the cross country and everyone's calling him Puke Boy, it's so funny | Do not spread the nickname; it is unkind and he may have been unwell | Spread it, because nicknames help people toughen up | Spread it, since it is true that he was sick | Spread it, but only in the group chat
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a warm-up undertaken prior to vigorous exercise. | To render the muscles warmer and readier for exertion, thereby diminishing the risk of strain | To exhaust the muscles before the commencement of the game | To ensure that no water need be consumed during the game | To render protective equipment unnecessary
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the injury most frequently sustained in skating and skateboarding? | The fracture of the wrist, which wrist guards serve to prevent | The fracture of the skull, which wrist guards serve to prevent | The sprain of the neck, which knee pads serve to prevent | The injury of the teeth, which elbow pads serve to prevent
exam: It is given that a participant in a sport has sustained a blow to the head but declares that he feels well. Select the appropriate course of action. | He should cease playing and be examined, notwithstanding that he feels well | He should continue to play, since he reports no symptoms | He should resume play after drinking a quantity of water | He should continue but avoid heading the ball
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning sharp pain experienced during sport. | It constitutes a signal that injury may have occurred, and play should not continue through it | It constitutes a sign that the muscles are growing stronger | It is to be disregarded until the conclusion of the game | It signifies only that a warm-up was omitted
